• identify the sources of law and distinguish the civil-law and common-law traditions, including their practical consequences for business; 
• explain the core substantive frameworks a cross-border firm meets: product liability, intellectual property (foundations and frontiers), contracts and the CISG, data and privacy, labour, and competition; 
• describe corporate criminal liability (the ‘failure to prevent’ model), compliance, and how entity choice and group structure allocate risk; 
• recognise why the same conduct is treated differently across jurisdictions, and how far a state’s law can reach beyond its borders. Cognitive / Intellectual Skills (generic) – The student is expected to:

• analyse a genuinely contested legal-business problem from multiple perspectives and reach a defensible position; 
• evaluate competing arguments honestly, including constructing the strongest case for a view they reject; 
• synthesise several legal frameworks at once on a single realistic scenario; 
• exercise critical judgement over AI output — interrogate, verify, and disagree with reasons. Key Transferable Skills (generic) – The student is expected to:

• spot legal risk in cross-border business decisions and recognise when to escalate to counsel; 
• apply the correct framework to a fact pattern — which law governs, and which regulators can reach the company; 
• draft and defend a reasoned board decision under time pressure; 
• structure a compliance-aware response to a corporate crisis, integrating the term’s frameworks.
